12. How to explain your research contribution to the evaluator

Your project should not claim that it has already solved every limitation of the existing paper. A stronger and more defensible explanation is:

Proposed contribution: UrbanEnergy Insight extends the analytical motivation of existing urban residential energy research into an integrated software workflow. It combines validated energy-data ingestion, configurable geographic analysis, contextual benchmarking, explainable high-consumption detection, evidence-based planning, and scenario estimation. Its intended advantage is accessibility and practical decision support, while its ability to generalize across countries and regions must be established through evaluation on suitable datasets.

You can also organize your future scope into three phases:

Phase 1 — MVP: CSV validation, persistent energy records, three login roles, analytics, geographic filters, high-consumption detection and planning actions.
Phase 2 — Research extension: Contextual benchmarking, weather normalization, explainable prediction and scenario analysis.
Phase 3 — Validation and expansion: Evaluate across multiple cities, states, territories, islands and countries; measure data-transferability limitations; integrate additional resource types and reliable external data sources.

My final recommendation: Keep the three logins and the redesigned UI. Add configurable geography, data-quality reporting, fair benchmarking and evidence-based explanations to the core application. Implement the simple scenario calculator if possible. Treat advanced machine learning and weather normalization as conditional features, because an incomplete or unvalidated model would weaken rather than strengthen your research claim.